# Async/await over callbacks

When writing asynchronous code, **always use `async`/`await`** instead of callback-based patterns.

Do **not** use:

- Callback parameters (`(err, result) => {}`)
- Nested `.then()` chains when `async`/`await` is clearer
- `new Promise((resolve, reject) => { ... })` wrappers around APIs that already return promises

## Examples

```javascript
// ❌ BAD — callback
fs.readFile(path, "utf8", (err, data) => {
  if (err) return handleError(err);
  processData(data);
});

// ✅ GOOD — async/await with promisified API
const data = await fs.promises.readFile(path, "utf8");
processData(data);
```

```javascript
// ❌ BAD — promise chain
getUser(id)
  .then((user) => getOrders(user.id))
  .then((orders) => renderOrders(orders))
  .catch(handleError);

// ✅ GOOD — async/await
try {
  const user = await getUser(id);
  const orders = await getOrders(user.id);
  renderOrders(orders);
} catch (err) {
  handleError(err);
}
```

```javascript
// ❌ BAD — manual Promise wrapper around an async API
function fetchData(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    axios.get(url).then(resolve).catch(reject);
  });
}

// ✅ GOOD — return the promise directly or use await
async function fetchData(url) {
  const { data } = await axios.get(url);
  return data;
}
```

## Refactoring existing code

If you touch a file that still uses callbacks or unnecessary `.then()` chains, refactor to `async`/`await` in the same change when the scope is small and safe.
